on" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057928/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129657" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "6.37"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057928/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd56eab8b5bc9f63d984b6c9ea8be9d43a273cac3967932c65823073636be38c" "basis" 1859 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057928/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129657" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6.37"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057928/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd56eab8b5bc9f63d984b6c9ea8be9d43a273cac3967932c65823073636be38c" "basis" 1859 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057928/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-98555" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{9}{x+2y-z}.$ Find the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(2,1,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057928/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd56eab8b5bc9f63d984b6c9ea8be9d43a273cac3967932c65823073636be38c" "basis" 1859 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057928/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-98555" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(2,1,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(2,1,1)+∇f(2,1,1)\\cdot ⟨x-2,y-1,z-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f,$ we have that $f(2,1,1)=3.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x} & =-\\frac{9}{(x+2y-z)^{2}}\\,⇒\\,{f}_{x}(2,1,1)=-\\frac{9}{(2+2(1)-1)^{2}}=-1, \\\\ {f}_{y} & =-\\frac{18}{(x+2y-z)^{2}}\\,⇒\\,{f}_{y}(2,1,1)=-\\frac{18}{(2+2(1)-1)^{2}}=-2, \\\\ {f}_{z} & =\\frac{9}{(x+2y-z)^{2}}\\,⇒\\,{f}_{z}(2,1,1)=\\frac{9}{(2+2(1)-1)^{2}}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(2,1,1)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(2,1,1) & =⟨{f}_{x}(2,1,1),{f}_{y}(2,1,1),{f}_{z}(2,1,1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,-2,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(2,1,1)+∇f(2,1,1)\\cdot ⟨x-2,y-1,z-1⟩ \\\\  & =3+⟨-1,-2,1⟩\\cdot ⟨x-2,y-1,z-1⟩ \\\\  & =3-1(x-2)-2(y-1)+1(z-1) \\\\  & =6-x-2y+z.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057928/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd56eab8b5bc9f63d984b6c9ea8be9d43a273cac3967932c65823073636be38c" "basis" 1859 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057928/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-98555" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057928/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5ec23d17fb67e890f41a65ae1ac28efe96d7fe546dc24470d93799900dc2cff6" "basis" 1859 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057928/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-98555" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057928/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd56eab8b5bc9f63d984b6c9ea8be9d43a273cac3967932c65823073636be38c" "basis" 1859 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057928/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-98555" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "f(x,y,z)≈6+x+2y-2z"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057928/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd56eab8b5bc9f63d984b6c9ea8be9d43a273cac3967932c65823073636be38c" "basis" 1859 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057928/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-98555" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "f(x,y,z)≈6-x+y-z"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057928/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd56eab8b5bc9f63d984b6c9ea8be9d43a273cac3967932c65823073636be38c" "basis" 1859 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057928/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-98555" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "f(x,y,z)≈6-x-2y+z"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057928/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd56eab8b5bc9f63d984b6c9ea8be9d43a273cac3967932c65823073636be38c" "basis" 1859 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057928/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-98555" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "f(x,y,z)≈8+x-2y-z"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057928/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd56eab8b5bc9f63d984b6c9ea8be9d43a273cac3967932c65823073636be38c" "basis" 1859 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057928/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-98555" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "f(x,y,z)≈8-x+y+z"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057928/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd56eab8b5bc9f63d984b6c9ea8be9d43a273cac3967932c65823073636be38c" "basis" 1859 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057928/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-8674" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f(x,y)$ be a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(1,1) = 1, \\quad f_x(x,y) = 2xy, \\quad f_y(x,y) = x^2.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,1),$ estimate the value of $f(1.1, 0.9).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057928/example-8674.json" "file_sha256" "02c17a7a0827987d6cde328e9e8812e729b5dfdc5d7b893e64665d14a142c063" "basis" 1859 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057928/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-8674" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y) \\approx f(a,b) + \\nabla f(a,b)\\cdot \\langle x-a, y-b \\rangle.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(1,1)+∇f(1,1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-1⟩ \\\\  & =f(1,1)+⟨{f}_{x}(1,1),{f}_{y}(1,1)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-1⟩ \\\\  & =1+⟨{f}_{x}(1,1),{f}_{y}(1,1)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find $f_x(1,1)$ and $f_y(1,1)$ using the given partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,1) & =2(1)(1)=2 \\\\ {f}_{y}(1,1) & =(1)^{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y) \\approx 1 + \\langle 2, 1 \\rangle \\cdot \\langle x-1, y-1 \\rangle.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can estimate $f(1.1, 0.9)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x= 1.1$ and $y=0.9,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.1,0.9) & ≈1+⟨2,1⟩\\cdot ⟨1.1-1,0.9-1⟩ \\\\  & =1+⟨2,1⟩\\cdot ⟨0.1,-0.1⟩ \\\\  & =1+0.2-0.1 \\\\  & =1.1\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057928/example-8674.json" "file_sha256" "02c17a7a0827987d6cde328e9e8812e729b5dfdc5d7b893e64665d14a142c063" "basis" 1859 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057928/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-8692" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that $f(x,y, z)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(1,2,5)=2, \\quad  f_x(1,2,5)=4, \\quad  f_y(1,2,5)=1, \\quad f_z(1,2,5)=-2.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,2,5)$ estimate the value of $f(1.01,2.02,4.95).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057928/example-8692.json" "file_sha256" "8f3c9c3e44194885133e1a735c07ab425db156ffc3d8abbee957f83cda4d3b19" "basis" 1859 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057928/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-8692" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b, c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) \\approx f(a,b,c) + \\nabla f(a,b,c)\\cdot \\langle x-a, y-b,z-c \\rangle.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,2,5)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(1,2,5)+∇f(1,2,5)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-2,z-5⟩ \\\\  & =f(1,2,5)+⟨{f}_{x}(1,2,5),{f}_{y}(1,2,5),{f}_{z}(1,2,5)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-2,z-5⟩ \\\\  & =2+⟨4,1,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-2,z-5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(1.01,2.02,4.95)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x= 1.01,$ $y=2.02,$ and $z=4.95$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.01,2.02,4.95) & ≈2+⟨4,1,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨1.01-1,2.02-2,4.95-5⟩ \\\\  & =2+⟨4,1,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨0.01,0.02,-0.05⟩ \\\\  & =2+0.04+0.02+0.1 \\\\  & =2.16\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057928/example-8692.json" "file_sha256" "8f3c9c3e44194885133e1a735c07ab425db156ffc3d8abbee957f83cda4d3b19" "basis" 1859 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057928/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-3162" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f(x,y, z)= xyz+y^2.$ Find the best linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(3, -1, 1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057928/example-3162.json" "file_sha256" "40aaefcd7796e4e469957d5e8a0a040fe21af50befe93de04bcb323d034af8d9" "basis" 1859 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057928/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-3162" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) \\approx f(a,b,c) + \\nabla f(a,b,c)\\cdot \\langle x-a, y-b, z-c \\rangle.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(3, -1, 1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(3,-1,1)+∇f(3,-1,1)\\cdot ⟨x-3,y+1,z-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f,$ we have that $f(3, -1, 1)= -2.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x} & =yz\\, &  & ⇒\\, &  & {f}_{x}(3,-1,1)=-1, \\\\ {f}_{y} & =xz+2y\\, &  & ⇒\\, &  & {f}_{y}(3,-1,1)=1 \\\\ {f}_{z} & =xy\\, &  & ⇒\\, &  & {f}_{z}(3,-1,1)=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(3, -1,1)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(3,-1,1) & =⟨-1,1,-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(3,-1,1)+∇f(3,-1,1)\\cdot ⟨x-3,y+1,z-1⟩ \\\\  & =-2+⟨-1,1,-3⟩\\cdot ⟨x-3,y+1,z-1⟩ \\\\  & =-2-(x-3)+(y+1)-3(z-1) \\\\  & =-2-x+3+y+1-3z+3 \\\\  & =5-x+y-3z.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057928/example-3162.json" "file_sha256" "40aaefcd7796e4e469957d5e8a0a040fe21af50befe93de04bcb323d034af8d9" "basis" 1859 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057928/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-98326" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that $f(x,y)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(2,1)=2,\\,{f}_{x}(2,1)=-1,\\,{f}_{y}(2,1)=-1.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(2,1),$ estimate the value of $f(1.98,0.94).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-98326" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(2,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(2,1)+∇f(2,1)\\cdot ⟨x-2,y-1⟩ \\\\  & =f(2,1)+⟨{f}_{x}(2,1),{f}_{y}(2,1)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-2,y-1⟩ \\\\  & =2+⟨-1,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨x-2,y-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(1.98,0.94)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=1.98$ and $y=0.94,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.98,0.94) & ≈2+⟨-1,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨1.98-2,0.94-1⟩ \\\\  & =2+⟨-1,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨-0.02,-0.06⟩ \\\\  & =2+(-1)(-0.02)+(-1)(-0.06) \\\\  & =2+0.02+0.06 \\\\  & =2.08\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-98326" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "51de299bea2d902584895a858dbc937266b8999e01db0178d19d05ba1deaafde"} {"question" "q-98326" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-98326" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1.92"] ["math" "1.96"] ["math" "2.06"] ["math" "2.08"] ["math" "2.12"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-98326" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2.08"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-129637" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f(x,y)$ be a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(1,-1)=-2,\\,{f}_{x}(x,y)=2xy-{y}^{2},\\,{f}_{y}(x,y)={x}^{2}-2xy.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,-1),$ estimate the value of $f(0.95,-1.04).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-129637" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,-1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(1,-1)+∇f(1,-1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-(-1)⟩ \\\\  & ≈f(1,-1)+⟨{f}_{x}(1,-1),{f}_{y}(1,-1)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1⟩ \\\\  & ≈-2+⟨{f}_{x}(1,-1),{f}_{y}(1,-1)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find ${f}_{x}(1,-1)$ and ${f}_{y}(1,-1)$ using the given partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,-1) & =2(1)(-1)-(-1)^{2}=-3 \\\\ {f}_{y}(1,-1) & ={1}^{2}-2(1)(-1)=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈-2+⟨-3,3⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can estimate $f(0.95,-1.04)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=0.95$ and $y=-1.04,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0.95,-1.04) & ≈-2+⟨-3,3⟩\\cdot ⟨0.95-1,-1.04+1⟩ \\\\  & =-2+⟨-3,3⟩\\cdot ⟨-0.05,-0.04⟩ \\\\  & =-2-3(-0.05)+3(-0.04) \\\\  & =-2+0.15-0.12 \\\\  & =-1.97\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-129637" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "51de299bea2d902584895a858dbc937266b8999e01db0178d19d05ba1deaafde"} {"question" "q-129637" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-129637" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1.94"] ["math" "-1.97"] ["math" "-1.99"] ["math" "-2.08"] ["math" "-2.11"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-129637" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1.97"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-129632" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that $f(x,y)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(1,-1)=-3,\\,{f}_{x}(1,-1)=-6,\\,{f}_{y}(1,-1)=3.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,-1),$ estimate the value of $f(1.01,-0.99).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-129632" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,-1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(1,-1)+∇f(1,-1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-(-1)⟩ \\\\  & =f(1,-1)+⟨{f}_{x}(1,-1),{f}_{y}(1,-1)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1⟩ \\\\  & =-3+⟨-6,3⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(1.01,-0.99)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=1.01$ and $y=-0.99,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.01,-0.99) & ≈-3+⟨-6,3⟩\\cdot ⟨1.01-1,-0.99+1⟩ \\\\  & =-3+⟨-6,3⟩\\cdot ⟨0.01,0.01⟩ \\\\  & =-3-6(0.01)+3(0.01) \\\\  & =-3-0.06+0.03 \\\\  & =-3.03\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-129632" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "51de299bea2d902584895a858dbc937266b8999e01db0178d19d05ba1deaafde"} {"question" "q-129632" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-129632" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2.91"] ["math" "-2.97"] ["math" "-3.03"] ["math" "-3.06"] ["math" "-3.09"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-129632" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-3.03"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-129634" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that $f(x,y)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(1,-1)=-2,\\,{f}_{x}(1,-1)=1,\\,{f}_{y}(1,-1)=6.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,-1),$ estimate the value of $f(0.96,-0.95).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-129634" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,-1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(1,-1)+∇f(1,-1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-(-1)⟩ \\\\  & =f(1,-1)+⟨{f}_{x}(1,-1),{f}_{y}(1,-1)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1⟩ \\\\  & =-2+⟨1,6⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(0.96,-0.95)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=0.96$ and $y=-0.95,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0.96,-0.95) & ≈-2+⟨1,6⟩\\cdot ⟨0.96-1,-0.95+1⟩ \\\\  & =-2+⟨1,6⟩\\cdot ⟨-0.04,0.05⟩ \\\\  & =-2+1(-0.04)+6(0.05) \\\\  & =-2-0.04+0.3 \\\\  & =-1.74\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-129634" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "51de299bea2d902584895a858dbc937266b8999e01db0178d19d05ba1deaafde"} {"question" "q-129634" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-129634" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1.74"] ["math" "-1.85"] ["math" "-1.97"] ["math" "-2.04"] ["math" "-2.11"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-129634" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1.74"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-129636" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f(x,y)$ be a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(1,3)=4,\\,{f}_{x}(x,y)=2x+y,\\,{f}_{y}(x,y)=x.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,3),$ estimate the value of $f(1.04,3.03).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-129636" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,3)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(1,3)+∇f(1,3)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-3⟩ \\\\  & ≈f(1,3)+⟨{f}_{x}(1,3),{f}_{y}(1,3)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-3⟩ \\\\  & ≈4+⟨{f}_{x}(1,3),{f}_{y}(1,3)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find ${f}_{x}(1,3)$ and ${f}_{y}(1,3)$ using the given partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,3) & =2(1)+3=5 \\\\ {f}_{y}(1,3) & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈4+⟨5,1⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-3⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can estimate $f(1.04,3.03)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=1.04$ and $y=3.03,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.04,3.03) & ≈4+⟨5,1⟩\\cdot ⟨1.04-1,3.03-3⟩ \\\\  & =4+⟨5,1⟩\\cdot ⟨0.04,0.03⟩ \\\\  & =4+5(0.04)+1(0.03) \\\\  & =4+0.2+0.03 \\\\  & =4.23\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-129636" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "51de299bea2d902584895a858dbc937266b8999e01db0178d19d05ba1deaafde"} {"question" "q-129636" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-129636" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3.92"] ["math" "3.98"] ["math" "4.04"] ["math" "4.11"] ["math" "4.23"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-129636" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4.23"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-129657" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that $f(x,y,z)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(1,2,3)=6,\\,{f}_{x}(1,2,3)=6,\\,{f}_{y}(1,2,3)=3,\\,{f}_{z}(1,2,3)=2.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,2,3)$ estimate the value of $f(1.08,1.93,3.05)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-129657" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,2,3)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(1,2,3)+∇f(1,2,3)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-2,z-3⟩ \\\\  & =6+⟨{f}_{x}(1,2,3),{f}_{y}(1,2,3),{f}_{z}(1,2,3)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-2,z-3⟩ \\\\  & =6+⟨6,3,2⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-2,z-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(1.08,1.93,3.05)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=1.08,$ $y=1.93,$ and $z=3.05$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.08,1.93,3.05) & ≈6+⟨6,3,2⟩\\cdot ⟨1.08-1,1.93-2,3.05-3⟩ \\\\  & =6+⟨6,3,2⟩\\cdot ⟨0.08,-0.07,0.05⟩ \\\\  & =6+6(0.08)+3(-0.07)+2(0.05) \\\\  & =6+0.48-0.21+0.1 \\\\  & =6.37\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-129657" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "51de299bea2d902584895a858dbc937266b8999e01db0178d19d05ba1deaafde"} {"question" "q-129657" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-129657" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "5.82"] ["math" "5.95"] ["math" "6.09"] ["math" "6.22"] ["math" "6.37"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-129657" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6.37"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-98344" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that $f(x,y,z)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & f(-3,-1,-2)=1, &  & {f}_{x}(-3,-1,-2)=4, \\\\  & {f}_{y}(-3,-1,-2)=-3, &  & {f}_{z}(-3,-1,-2)=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(-3,-1,-2)$ estimate the value of $f(-3.04,-1.02,-2.03).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-98344" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(-3,-1,-2)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(-3,-1,-2)+∇f(-3,-1,-2)\\cdot ⟨x+3,y+1,z+2⟩ \\\\  & =f(-3,-1,-2) \\\\  & \\,+⟨{f}_{x}(-3,-1,-2),{f}_{y}(-3,-1,-2),{f}_{z}(-3,-1,-2)⟩\\cdot ⟨x+3,y+1,z+2⟩ \\\\  & =1+⟨4,-3,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨x+3,y+1,z+2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(-3.04,-1.02,-2.03)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=-3.04,$ $y=-1.02,$ and $z=-2.03$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3.04,-1.02,-2.03) & ≈1+⟨4,-3,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨-3.04+3,-1.02+1,-2.03+2⟩ \\\\  & =1+⟨4,-3,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨-0.04,-0.02,-0.03⟩ \\\\  & =1-0.16+0.06+0.06 \\\\  & =0.96\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-98344" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "51de299bea2d902584895a858dbc937266b8999e01db0178d19d05ba1deaafde"} {"question" "q-98344" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-98344" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0.72"] ["math" "0.84"] ["math" "0.96"] ["math" "1.04"] ["math" "1.28"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-98344" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.96"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-98348" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f(x,y,z)$ be a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,2,-3)=-6, & \\,{f}_{x}(x,y,z)=yz,\\,{f}_{y}(x,y,z)=xz,\\,{f}_{z}(x,y,z)=xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,2,-3),$ estimate the value of $f(1.01,1.95,-3.02).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-98348" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,2,-3)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(1,2,-3)+∇f(1,2,-3)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-2,z+3⟩ \\\\  & =-6+⟨{f}_{x}(1,2,-3),{f}_{y}(1,2,-3),{f}_{z}(1,2,-3)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-2,z+3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find ${f}_{x}(1,2,-3)$, ${f}_{y}(1,2,-3)$ and ${f}_{z}(1,2,-3),$ using the given partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,2,-3) & =2(-3)=-6 \\\\ {f}_{y}(1,2,-3) & =1(-3)=-3 \\\\ {f}_{z}(1,2,-3) & =1(2)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈-6+⟨-6,-3,2⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-2,z+3⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can estimate $f(1.01,1.95,-3.02)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=1.01,y=1.95$ and $z=-3.02,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.01,1.95,-3.02) & ≈-6+⟨-6,-3,2⟩\\cdot ⟨1.01-1,1.95-2,-3.02+3⟩ \\\\  & =-6+⟨-6,-3,2⟩\\cdot ⟨0.01,-0.05,-0.02⟩ \\\\  & =-6-0.06+0.15-0.04 \\\\  & =-5.95\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-98348" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "51de299bea2d902584895a858dbc937266b8999e01db0178d19d05ba1deaafde"} {"question" "q-98348" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-98348" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-5.87"] ["math" "-5.95"] ["math" "-5.98"] ["math" "-6.01"] ["math" "-6.12"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-98348" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-5.95"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-129661" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f(x,y,z)$ be a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(0,1,-1)=-2,\\,{f}_{x}(x,y,z)=z{e}^{x},\\,{f}_{y}(x,y,z)=-1,\\,{f}_{z}(x,y,z)={e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(0,1,-1),$ estimate the value of $f(0.01,1.01,-0.99).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-129661" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(0,1,-1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(0,1,-1)+∇f(0,1,-1)\\cdot ⟨x-0,y-1,z-(-1)⟩ \\\\  & ≈-2+⟨{f}_{x}(0,1,-1),{f}_{y}(0,1,-1),{f}_{z}(0,1,-1)⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y-1,z+1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find ${f}_{x}(0,1,-1)$, ${f}_{y}(0,1,-1)$ and ${f}_{z}(0,1,-1),$ using the given partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,1,-1) & =(-1){e}^{0}=-1 \\\\ {f}_{y}(0,1,-1) & =-1 \\\\ {f}_{z}(0,1,-1) & ={e}^{0}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈-2+⟨-1,-1,1⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y-1,z+1⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can estimate $f(0.01,1.01,-0.99)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=0.01,y=1.01$ and $y=-0.99,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0.01,1.01,-0.99) & ≈-2+⟨-1,-1,1⟩\\cdot ⟨0.01,1.01-1,-0.99+1⟩ \\\\  & ≈-2+⟨-1,-1,1⟩\\cdot ⟨0.01,0.01,0.01⟩ \\\\  & ≈-2-1(0.01)-1(0.01)+1(0.01) \\\\  & ≈-2-0.01-0.01+0.01 \\\\  & ≈-2.01\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-129661" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "51de299bea2d902584895a858dbc937266b8999e01db0178d19d05ba1deaafde"} {"question" "q-129661" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-129661" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1.97"] ["math" "-1.99"] ["math" "-2.01"] ["math" "-2.03"] ["math" "-2.04"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-129661" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2.01"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-129655" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that $f(x,y,z)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(-1,1,0)=-1,\\,{f}_{x}(-1,1,0)=1,\\,{f}_{y}(-1,1,0)=-2,\\,{f}_{z}(-1,1,0)=0.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(-1,1,0)$ estimate the value of $f(-1,1.01,-0.01)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-129655" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(-1,1,0)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(-1,1,0)+∇f(-1,1,0)\\cdot ⟨x-(-1),y-1,z-0⟩ \\\\  & =f(-1,1,0)+⟨{f}_{x}(-1,1,0),{f}_{y}(-1,1,0),{f}_{z}(-1,1,0)⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,y-1,z⟩ \\\\  & =-1+⟨1,-2,0⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,y-1,z⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(-1,1.01,-0.01)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=-1,$ $y=1.01,$ and $z=-0.01$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1,1.01,-0.01) & ≈-1+⟨1,-2,0⟩\\cdot ⟨-1+1,1.01-1,-0.01⟩ \\\\  & =-1+⟨1,-2,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0.01,-0.01⟩ \\\\  & =-1+1(0)-2(0.01)+0(-0.01) \\\\  & =-1+0-0.02+0 \\\\  & =-1.02\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-129655" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "51de299bea2d902584895a858dbc937266b8999e01db0178d19d05ba1deaafde"} {"question" "q-129655" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-129655" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-0.97"] ["math" "-0.98"] ["math" "-1.01"] ["math" "-1.02"] ["math" "-1.04"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-129655" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1.02"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-36747" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x+y).$ Find the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(0,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-36747" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(0,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(0,1)+∇f(0,1)\\cdot ⟨x,y-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f,$ we have that $f(0,1)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x} & =\\frac{1}{1+x+y}\\, &  & ⇒\\, & {f}_{x}(0,1)=\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ {f}_{y} & =\\frac{1}{1+x+y} &  & ⇒\\, & {f}_{y}(0,1)=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(0,1)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(0,1) & =⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(0,1)+∇f(0,1)\\cdot ⟨x,y-1⟩ \\\\  & ≈\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y-1⟩ \\\\  & ≈\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{y-1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-36747" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "51de299bea2d902584895a858dbc937266b8999e01db0178d19d05ba1deaafde"} {"question" "q-36747" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-36747" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "f(x,y)≈\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{y-1}{2}"] ["math" "f(x,y)≈\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{x}{2}-\\frac{y-1}{2}"] ["math" "f(x,y)≈\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{y-1}{3}"] ["math" "f(x,y)≈\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-1+x+y"] ["math" "f(x,y)≈\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{y-1}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-36747" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "f(x,y)≈\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{y-1}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-98554" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f(x,y,z)=xy-\\operatorname{ln}⁡z.$ Find the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,1,e).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-98554" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,1,e)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(1,1,e)+∇f(1,1,e)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-1,z-e⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f,$ we have that $f(1,1,e)=0.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x} & =y\\, &  & ⇒\\, & {f}_{x}(1,1,e)=1, \\\\ {f}_{y} & =x\\, &  & ⇒\\, & {f}_{y}(1,1,e)=1, \\\\ {f}_{z} & =-\\frac{1}{z}\\, &  & ⇒\\, & {f}_{z}(1,1,e)=-\\frac{1}{e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(1,1,e)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(1,1,e) & =⟨1,1,-\\frac{1}{e}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(1,1,e)+∇f(1,1,e)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-1,z-e⟩ \\\\  & =0+⟨1,1,-\\frac{1}{e}⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-1,z-e⟩ \\\\  & =0+1(x-1)+1(y-1)-\\frac{1}{e}(z-e) \\\\  & =-1+x+y-\\frac{z}{e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-98554" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "51de299bea2d902584895a858dbc937266b8999e01db0178d19d05ba1deaafde"} {"question" "q-98554" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-98554" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "f(x,y,z)≈-1+x+y-\\frac{z}{e}"] ["math" "f(x,y,z)≈-1-x+y-2z"] ["math" "f(x,y,z)≈-3+x-y+\\frac{z}{e}"] ["math" "f(x,y,z)≈-3+x-y-2z"] ["math" "f(x,y,z)≈1-x+y-\\frac{z}{e}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-98554" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "f(x,y,z)≈-1+x+y-\\frac{z}{e}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-98551" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f(x,y)=2\\sqrt{xy}.$ Find the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(2,8).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-98551" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(2,8)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(2,8)+∇f(2,8)\\cdot ⟨x-2,y-8⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f,$ we have that $f(2,8)=8.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x} & =\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{xy}}\\, &  & ⇒\\, & {f}_{x}(2,8)=\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{2\\cdot 8}}=2, \\\\ {f}_{y} & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{xy}} &  & ⇒\\, & {f}_{y}(2,8)=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2\\cdot 8}}=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(2,8)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(2,8) & =⟨2,\\frac{1}{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(2,8)+∇\\,f(2,8)\\cdot ⟨x-2,\\,y-8⟩ \\\\  & =8+⟨2,\\frac{1}{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨x-2,\\,y-8⟩ \\\\  & =8+2(x-2)+\\frac{1}{2}(y-8) \\\\  & =2x+\\frac{y}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-98551" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "51de299bea2d902584895a858dbc937266b8999e01db0178d19d05ba1deaafde"} {"question" "q-98551" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-98551" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "f(x,y)≈16+2x+\\frac{y}{2}"] ["math" "f(x,y)≈16+2x+y"] ["math" "f(x,y)≈2x+\\frac{y}{2}"] ["math" "f(x,y)≈\\frac{x}{2}+2y"] ["math" "f(x,y)≈\\frac{x}{2}+y"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-98551" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "f(x,y)≈2x+\\frac{y}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-98555" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{9}{x+2y-z}.$ Find the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(2,1,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-98555" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(2,1,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(2,1,1)+∇f(2,1,1)\\cdot ⟨x-2,y-1,z-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f,$ we have that $f(2,1,1)=3.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x} & =-\\frac{9}{(x+2y-z)^{2}}\\,⇒\\,{f}_{x}(2,1,1)=-\\frac{9}{(2+2(1)-1)^{2}}=-1, \\\\ {f}_{y} & =-\\frac{18}{(x+2y-z)^{2}}\\,⇒\\,{f}_{y}(2,1,1)=-\\frac{18}{(2+2(1)-1)^{2}}=-2, \\\\ {f}_{z} & =\\frac{9}{(x+2y-z)^{2}}\\,⇒\\,{f}_{z}(2,1,1)=\\frac{9}{(2+2(1)-1)^{2}}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(2,1,1)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(2,1,1) & =⟨{f}_{x}(2,1,1),{f}_{y}(2,1,1),{f}_{z}(2,1,1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,-2,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(2,1,1)+∇f(2,1,1)\\cdot ⟨x-2,y-1,z-1⟩ \\\\  & =3+⟨-1,-2,1⟩\\cdot ⟨x-2,y-1,z-1⟩ \\\\  & =3-1(x-2)-2(y-1)+1(z-1) \\\\  & =6-x-2y+z.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-98555" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "51de299bea2d902584895a858dbc937266b8999e01db0178d19d05ba1deaafde"} {"question" "q-98555" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-98555" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "f(x,y,z)≈6+x+2y-2z"] ["math" "f(x,y,z)≈6-x+y-z"] ["math" "f(x,y,z)≈6-x-2y+z"] ["math" "f(x,y,z)≈8+x-2y-z"] ["math" "f(x,y,z)≈8-x+y+z"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-98555" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "f(x,y,z)≈6-x-2y+z"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-129748" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f(x,y,z)=xyz.$ Find the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,-1,-1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-129748" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,-1,-1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(1,-1,-1)+∇f(1,-1,-1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1,z+1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f,$ we have that $f(1,-1,-1)=1.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x} & =yz\\,⇒\\,{f}_{x}(1,-1,-1)=1, \\\\ {f}_{y} & =xz\\,⇒\\,{f}_{y}(1,-1,-1)=-1, \\\\ {f}_{z} & =xy\\,⇒\\,{f}_{z}(1,-1,-1)=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(1,-1,-1)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(1,-1,-1) & =⟨{f}_{x}(1,-1,-1),{f}_{y}(1,-1,-1),{f}_{z}(1,-1,-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(1,-1,-1)+∇f(1,-1,-1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1,z+1⟩ \\\\  & ≈1+⟨1,-1,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1,z+1⟩ \\\\  & ≈1+(x-1)-(y+1)-(z+1) \\\\  & ≈-2+x-y-z.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-129748" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "51de299bea2d902584895a858dbc937266b8999e01db0178d19d05ba1deaafde"} {"question" "q-129748" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-129748" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "f(x,y,z)≈-2+x-y-z"] ["math" "f(x,y,z)≈-2y+z"] ["math" "f(x,y,z)≈1+x+z"] ["math" "f(x,y,z)≈2+x+2y"] ["math" "f(x,y,z)≈2+x+y+z"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "q-129748" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "f(x,y,z)≈-2+x-y-z"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/state.json" "file_sha256" "85ba2c1ccb585556465b620cb07d267dd4d8590f42d9163cc6ec22a39c98f458"} {"question" "e-8674" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f(x,y)$ be a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(1,1) = 1, \\quad f_x(x,y) = 2xy, \\quad f_y(x,y) = x^2.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,1),$ estimate the value of $f(1.1, 0.9).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/example-8674.json" "file_sha256" "02c17a7a0827987d6cde328e9e8812e729b5dfdc5d7b893e64665d14a142c063"} {"question" "e-8674" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y) \\approx f(a,b) + \\nabla f(a,b)\\cdot \\langle x-a, y-b \\rangle.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(1,1)+∇f(1,1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-1⟩ \\\\  & =f(1,1)+⟨{f}_{x}(1,1),{f}_{y}(1,1)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-1⟩ \\\\  & =1+⟨{f}_{x}(1,1),{f}_{y}(1,1)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find $f_x(1,1)$ and $f_y(1,1)$ using the given partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,1) & =2(1)(1)=2 \\\\ {f}_{y}(1,1) & =(1)^{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y) \\approx 1 + \\langle 2, 1 \\rangle \\cdot \\langle x-1, y-1 \\rangle.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can estimate $f(1.1, 0.9)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x= 1.1$ and $y=0.9,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.1,0.9) & ≈1+⟨2,1⟩\\cdot ⟨1.1-1,0.9-1⟩ \\\\  & =1+⟨2,1⟩\\cdot ⟨0.1,-0.1⟩ \\\\  & =1+0.2-0.1 \\\\  & =1.1\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/example-8674.json" "file_sha256" "02c17a7a0827987d6cde328e9e8812e729b5dfdc5d7b893e64665d14a142c063"} {"question" "e-8692" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that $f(x,y, z)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(1,2,5)=2, \\quad  f_x(1,2,5)=4, \\quad  f_y(1,2,5)=1, \\quad f_z(1,2,5)=-2.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,2,5)$ estimate the value of $f(1.01,2.02,4.95).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/example-8692.json" "file_sha256" "8f3c9c3e44194885133e1a735c07ab425db156ffc3d8abbee957f83cda4d3b19"} {"question" "e-8692" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b, c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) \\approx f(a,b,c) + \\nabla f(a,b,c)\\cdot \\langle x-a, y-b,z-c \\rangle.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,2,5)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(1,2,5)+∇f(1,2,5)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-2,z-5⟩ \\\\  & =f(1,2,5)+⟨{f}_{x}(1,2,5),{f}_{y}(1,2,5),{f}_{z}(1,2,5)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-2,z-5⟩ \\\\  & =2+⟨4,1,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-2,z-5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(1.01,2.02,4.95)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x= 1.01,$ $y=2.02,$ and $z=4.95$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.01,2.02,4.95) & ≈2+⟨4,1,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨1.01-1,2.02-2,4.95-5⟩ \\\\  & =2+⟨4,1,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨0.01,0.02,-0.05⟩ \\\\  & =2+0.04+0.02+0.1 \\\\  & =2.16\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/example-8692.json" "file_sha256" "8f3c9c3e44194885133e1a735c07ab425db156ffc3d8abbee957f83cda4d3b19"} {"question" "e-3162" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f(x,y, z)= xyz+y^2.$ Find the best linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(3, -1, 1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/example-3162.json" "file_sha256" "40aaefcd7796e4e469957d5e8a0a040fe21af50befe93de04bcb323d034af8d9"} {"question" "e-3162" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) \\approx f(a,b,c) + \\nabla f(a,b,c)\\cdot \\langle x-a, y-b, z-c \\rangle.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(3, -1, 1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(3,-1,1)+∇f(3,-1,1)\\cdot ⟨x-3,y+1,z-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f,$ we have that $f(3, -1, 1)= -2.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x} & =yz\\, &  & ⇒\\, &  & {f}_{x}(3,-1,1)=-1, \\\\ {f}_{y} & =xz+2y\\, &  & ⇒\\, &  & {f}_{y}(3,-1,1)=1 \\\\ {f}_{z} & =xy\\, &  & ⇒\\, &  & {f}_{z}(3,-1,1)=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(3, -1,1)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(3,-1,1) & =⟨-1,1,-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(3,-1,1)+∇f(3,-1,1)\\cdot ⟨x-3,y+1,z-1⟩ \\\\  & =-2+⟨-1,1,-3⟩\\cdot ⟨x-3,y+1,z-1⟩ \\\\  & =-2-(x-3)+(y+1)-3(z-1) \\\\  & =-2-x+3+y+1-3z+3 \\\\  & =5-x+y-3z.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068301/example-3162.json" "file_sha256" "40aaefcd7796e4e469957d5e8a0a040fe21af50befe93de04bcb323d034af8d9"}] "usage" {"q-98348" [] "q-129634" [] "q-98555" [] "q-36747" [] "e-8692" [] "e-8674" [] "q-129657" [] "e-3162" []} "basis" 2292 "retractions" [[17592186124924 :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nLet $f(x, y) = \\ln (1 + x + y)$. Find the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(0, 1)$."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186122567 17592186122568 17592186122569 17592186122570 17592186122571 17592186335313 17592186335314 17592186335315 17592186335316 17592186335317 17592186335320 17592186335321 17592186335322 17592186335323 17592186335324 17592186335369 17592186335370 17592186335371 17592186335372 17592186335373 17592186335390 17592186335391 17592186335392 17592186335393 17592186335394] "immutable_fields" [[17592186122566 :answer-field/correct] [17592186122566 :answer-field/id] [17592186122566 :answer-field/key] [17592186122566 :answer-field/type] [17592186335312 :answer-field/correct] [17592186335312 :answer-field/id] [17592186335312 :answer-field/key] [17592186335312 :answer-field/type] [17592186335319 :answer-field/correct] [17592186335319 :answer-field/id] [1759